1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,600
Welcome to Topic 9.

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,050
1, Performance Monitoring.

3
00:00:03,300 --> 00:00:07,700
Sooner or later someone will tell you the
server is slow.

4
00:00:07,950 --> 00:00:13,150
This topic gives you the tools to answer
with facts instead of guesses.

5
00:00:13,400 --> 00:00:19,000
You will learn why baselines matter, which
counters to watch for CPU, memory, disk

6
00:00:19,250 --> 00:00:22,850
and network, and what the usual warning
levels are.

7
00:00:23,100 --> 00:00:28,300
You will find a busy process with Task
Manager and Resource Monitor, watch

8
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:33,750
counters in Performance Monitor, record
them with a data collector set, and read

9
00:00:34,000 --> 00:00:36,000
them from PowerShell with Get-Counter.

10
00:00:36,250 --> 00:00:37,850
Why monitor at all?

11
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:41,300
Because a number on its own means little.

12
00:00:41,550 --> 00:00:43,550
Is forty percent CPU bad?

13
00:00:43,800 --> 00:00:47,400
It depends on what is normal for that
server.

14
00:00:47,650 --> 00:00:52,850
A baseline is a recording of normal, made
while the server is healthy.

15
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,900
Weeks one to four set the baseline.

16
00:00:56,150 --> 00:00:59,750
From week five, CPU climbs a little every
week.

17
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,400
That trend tells you the server will run
out of capacity, so you can plan early.

18
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:11,450
Monitoring also shows whether you keep
your service level agreement, your promise

19
00:01:11,700 --> 00:01:14,500
to users about response time or uptime.

20
00:01:14,750 --> 00:01:17,150
Server+ covers baselines and SLAs too.

21
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:20,200
Every server has four subsystems to watch.

22
00:01:20,450 --> 00:01:22,050
The CPU runs instructions.

23
00:01:22,300 --> 00:01:25,500
Memory holds the programs and data in use.

24
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:28,950
The disk stores everything, including the
paging file.

25
00:01:29,200 --> 00:01:32,400
The network moves data to and from
clients.

26
00:01:32,650 --> 00:01:34,250
They affect each other.

27
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:39,700
When memory runs short, Windows moves
pages of memory out to the disk.

28
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:41,550
That is called paging.

29
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:47,000
Now the disk is busy, disk latency rises,
and the server feels slow.

30
00:01:47,250 --> 00:01:52,050
It looks like a disk problem, but the real
cause is memory.

31
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:57,100
A bottleneck is the one resource that
holds back all the others.

32
00:01:57,350 --> 00:02:01,750
A counter is one measurement that Windows
keeps up to date.

33
00:02:02,000 --> 00:02:04,000
Here are seven to know.

34
00:02:04,250 --> 00:02:09,450
Look closer when CPU stays over about
eighty to eighty-five percent for minutes.

35
00:02:09,700 --> 00:02:14,900
When Available MBytes falls under ten
percent of RAM, memory is getting short,

36
00:02:15,150 --> 00:02:17,950
and under five hundred megabytes is
critical.

37
00:02:18,200 --> 00:02:23,000
Pages per second that stays high while
memory is low means paging.

38
00:02:23,250 --> 00:02:27,650
A disk read that takes more than
twenty-five milliseconds is slow.

39
00:02:27,900 --> 00:02:32,700
Low percent Idle Time and a long queue
mean a busy disk.

40
00:02:32,950 --> 00:02:38,150
Treat these as guidance from Microsoft,
not hard limits, and compare with your

41
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:39,600
baseline.

42
00:02:39,850 --> 00:02:42,250
Task Manager is the quickest look.

43
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:44,100
Press Control, Shift, Escape.

44
00:02:44,350 --> 00:02:50,750
On the Processes page, select the CPU or
Memory column heading to sort, and the top

45
00:02:51,000 --> 00:02:52,600
process becomes your suspect.

46
00:02:52,850 --> 00:02:57,650
The Performance page shows live graphs for
CPU, memory, disk and Ethernet.

47
00:02:57,900 --> 00:03:01,900
On the CPU page, note the number of
logical processors.

48
00:03:02,150 --> 00:03:08,150
That matters later: a process that loops
on one logical processor fills just one of

49
00:03:08,400 --> 00:03:11,200
them, so the total may look moderate.

50
00:03:11,450 --> 00:03:14,650
Remember that Task Manager shows the
present moment.

51
00:03:14,900 --> 00:03:17,300
One look is not a baseline.

52
00:03:17,550 --> 00:03:22,750
The Details page lists every process
separately, with its process ID, or PID.

53
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:26,600
Many processes share a name, like svchost
or powershell.

54
00:03:26,850 --> 00:03:32,050
To tell them apart, right-click a column
heading, choose Select columns, and tick

55
00:03:32,300 --> 00:03:33,500
Command line.

56
00:03:33,750 --> 00:03:39,350
Now you see the full path and the
arguments each process was started with.

57
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:45,200
Here, one powershell process is running a
loop that counts up forever and never

58
00:03:45,450 --> 00:03:46,650
stops.

59
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:48,500
That is your culprit.

60
00:03:48,750 --> 00:03:51,150
End task stops that one PID.

61
00:03:51,400 --> 00:03:56,200
But first find out what started it, or it
may come back.

62
00:03:56,450 --> 00:03:58,450
PowerShell does the same job.

63
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:03,900
Get-Process, sorted by the CPU property,
lists the processes that have used the

64
00:04:04,150 --> 00:04:05,350
most processor time.

65
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:11,200
Note that CPU here is total seconds since
the process started, not a percentage.

66
00:04:11,450 --> 00:04:16,650
To tell two powershell processes apart,
ask CIM for the Win32 Process class,

67
00:04:16,900 --> 00:04:20,100
filtered by name, and select ProcessId and
CommandLine.

68
00:04:20,350 --> 00:04:24,350
That is the same information as the Task
Manager column.

69
00:04:24,600 --> 00:04:29,000
Then Stop-Process with the ID ends it,
just like End task.

70
00:04:29,250 --> 00:04:34,850
This also works in a remote PowerShell
session, where there is no Task Manager

71
00:04:35,100 --> 00:04:36,300
window to click.

72
00:04:36,550 --> 00:04:40,950
Ending a busy process helps only until
something starts it again.

73
00:04:41,200 --> 00:04:43,200
So ask who started it.

74
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:46,650
Get-CimInstance Win32 Process gives each
process a ParentProcessId.

75
00:04:46,900 --> 00:04:48,900
Read the parent's command line.

76
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:53,950
A service host running the Schedule
service means Task Scheduler started it.

77
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:58,200
Look in the Task Scheduler Library, or run
Get-ScheduledTask and

78
00:04:58,450 --> 00:05:00,450
Get-ScheduledTaskInfo for the last result.

79
00:05:00,700 --> 00:05:04,700
Check services and their startup type too,
and startup apps.

80
00:05:04,950 --> 00:05:08,150
Fix the source first:
Disable-ScheduledTask, or disable the

81
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:09,600
service.

82
00:05:09,850 --> 00:05:11,450
Then end the process.

83
00:05:11,700 --> 00:05:13,300
Resource Monitor goes deeper.

84
00:05:13,550 --> 00:05:14,750
Type resmon.

85
00:05:15,000 --> 00:05:19,000
It has five tabs: Overview, CPU, Memory,
Disk and Network.

86
00:05:19,250 --> 00:05:26,050
Tick the check box next to a process, and
it stays selected on every tab, so the

87
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:28,700
lower tables show only that process.

88
00:05:28,950 --> 00:05:34,950
On the Disk tab, Disk Activity shows which
files it reads and writes, with response

89
00:05:35,200 --> 00:05:36,400
times.

90
00:05:36,650 --> 00:05:42,250
On the Network tab, Listening Ports shows
which process waits on which port: 3389

91
00:05:42,500 --> 00:05:46,500
for Remote Desktop, 445 for SMB and 5985
for WinRM.

92
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:48,750
Get-NetTCPConnection gives the same list.

93
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:51,000
For counters, open Performance Monitor.

94
00:05:51,250 --> 00:05:55,650
Type perfmon, then select Monitoring
Tools, Performance Monitor, and the green

95
00:05:55,900 --> 00:05:57,100
plus.

96
00:05:57,350 --> 00:06:02,550
In Add Counters, expand an object such as
PhysicalDisk and pick a counter.

97
00:06:02,800 --> 00:06:04,400
Then pick an instance.

98
00:06:04,650 --> 00:06:10,650
An instance is one copy of what the object
measures: one disk, one processor core,

99
00:06:10,900 --> 00:06:12,100
one network adapter.

100
00:06:12,350 --> 00:06:17,150
The underscore Total instance combines
them, which is a good first look.

101
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,000
Select Add, then OK.

102
00:06:19,250 --> 00:06:24,050
Tick Show description whenever you are not
sure what a counter means.

103
00:06:24,300 --> 00:06:26,300
Performance Monitor has three views.

104
00:06:26,550 --> 00:06:28,550
Press Control G to switch.

105
00:06:28,800 --> 00:06:34,800
Line is the default and shows about one
hundred seconds at one sample per second.

106
00:06:35,050 --> 00:06:37,850
Histogram bar shows one bar per counter.

107
00:06:38,100 --> 00:06:39,700
Report shows plain numbers.

108
00:06:39,950 --> 00:06:45,150
Under the line graph you see Last,
Average, Minimum, Maximum and Duration for

109
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:48,200
the counter you select in the legend.

110
00:06:48,450 --> 00:06:52,050
Windows Admin Center has a Performance
Monitor tool too.

111
00:06:52,300 --> 00:06:57,900
It offers the same counters in a browser,
for remote servers, with Line, Report,

112
00:06:58,150 --> 00:07:02,150
Min-Max and Heatmap graphs, and you can
save a workspace.

113
00:07:02,400 --> 00:07:05,200
Live graphs disappear when you close them.

114
00:07:05,450 --> 00:07:09,450
To record over time, you create a data
collector set.

115
00:07:09,700 --> 00:07:15,700
Under User Defined, select New, Data
Collector Set, give it a name such as SA1

116
00:07:15,950 --> 00:07:17,950
Baseline, and choose Create manually.

117
00:07:18,200 --> 00:07:22,600
Pick Performance counter, add your
counters, and set the sample interval.

118
00:07:22,850 --> 00:07:27,250
The wizard offers fifteen seconds; in the
lab you use five.

119
00:07:27,500 --> 00:07:31,100
The root directory is C, PerfLogs, Admin,
SA1 Baseline.

120
00:07:31,350 --> 00:07:36,150
Under System you also find two ready-made
sets, System Diagnostics and System

121
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:37,600
Performance.

122
00:07:37,850 --> 00:07:40,250
They run for about a minute.

123
00:07:40,500 --> 00:07:44,900
A set with no stop condition runs until
somebody stops it.

124
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:50,350
So open its Properties, select the Stop
Condition tab, and set an overall

125
00:07:50,600 --> 00:07:52,600
duration, for example five minutes.

126
00:07:52,850 --> 00:07:58,050
At one sample every five seconds, that is
about sixty samples per counter.

127
00:07:58,300 --> 00:08:02,300
You can predict that number because you
chose both values.

128
00:08:02,550 --> 00:08:07,750
Each run creates a new report under
Reports, User Defined, with the computer

129
00:08:08,000 --> 00:08:10,800
name, the date and a run number.

130
00:08:11,050 --> 00:08:17,850
Open it, and you get the same graph tools,
but on recorded data from the log file.

131
00:08:18,100 --> 00:08:20,500
In PowerShell, Get-Counter reads counters
directly.

132
00:08:20,750 --> 00:08:26,350
Put the counter paths in a variable, then
add SampleInterval for the seconds between

133
00:08:26,600 --> 00:08:29,000
samples and MaxSamples for how many.

134
00:08:29,250 --> 00:08:32,850
Without them you get one sample, one
second apart.

135
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:37,500
Each sample has one timestamp and a value
for every counter.

136
00:08:37,750 --> 00:08:42,150
For data collector sets there is a
command-line tool called logman.

137
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:48,800
logman create counter makes a set; dash si
sets the sample interval and dash rf how

138
00:08:49,050 --> 00:08:50,250
long it runs.

139
00:08:50,500 --> 00:08:55,300
logman start runs it, and logman query
shows its settings and status.

140
00:08:55,550 --> 00:08:57,550
Now put the clues together.

141
00:08:57,800 --> 00:09:03,400
Low Available MBytes, high Pages per
second and a busy disk point to memory.

142
00:09:03,650 --> 00:09:07,650
Find the process using the most memory, or
add RAM.

143
00:09:07,900 --> 00:09:13,500
CPU over eighty-five percent for minutes,
with one process on top, points to the

144
00:09:13,750 --> 00:09:14,950
CPU.

145
00:09:15,200 --> 00:09:18,000
Find that process and its command line.

146
00:09:18,250 --> 00:09:24,250
Slow disk reads with a long queue while
memory is fine point to the disk.

147
00:09:24,500 --> 00:09:27,700
Traffic near link speed points to the
network.

148
00:09:27,950 --> 00:09:33,150
And always check memory first, because a
memory shortage makes the disk look

149
00:09:33,400 --> 00:09:34,600
guilty.

150
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:36,450
Four mistakes to avoid.

151
00:09:36,700 --> 00:09:40,700
One: judging a server from one look at
Task Manager.

152
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:45,350
Record a data collector set and compare it
with the baseline.

153
00:09:45,600 --> 00:09:48,800
Two: buying faster disks when memory is
short.

154
00:09:49,050 --> 00:09:52,250
Check Available MBytes and Pages per
second first.

155
00:09:52,500 --> 00:09:55,700
Three: treating rules of thumb as hard
limits.

156
00:09:55,950 --> 00:10:01,550
Short spikes are normal; look for values
that stay high and differ from the

157
00:10:01,800 --> 00:10:03,000
baseline.

158
00:10:03,250 --> 00:10:05,250
Four: leaving a test running.

159
00:10:05,500 --> 00:10:11,500
Give every data collector set a stop
condition, and stop your CPU loop when you

160
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:12,950
finish the lab.

161
00:10:13,200 --> 00:10:14,800
Time to check yourself.

162
00:10:15,050 --> 00:10:20,650
First: SRV01 has four logical processors,
and one process loops on one of them.

163
00:10:20,900 --> 00:10:25,300
About what does percent Processor Time for
the Total instance show?

164
00:10:25,550 --> 00:10:31,550
Second: Available MBytes is low, Pages per
second is high, and a disk read takes

165
00:10:31,800 --> 00:10:33,000
forty milliseconds.

166
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:35,250
What do you fix first?

167
00:10:35,500 --> 00:10:41,100
Third: a data collector set samples every
five seconds and stops after five minutes.

168
00:10:41,350 --> 00:10:44,950
About how many samples does it record per
counter?

169
00:10:45,200 --> 00:10:47,600
Fourth: Details shows two powershell
processes.

170
00:10:47,850 --> 00:10:51,450
How do you tell which one runs the loop?

171
00:10:51,700 --> 00:10:53,700
Pause the video and answer.

172
00:10:53,950 --> 00:10:55,550
Here are the answers.

173
00:10:55,800 --> 00:10:57,400
One: about twenty-five percent.

174
00:10:57,650 --> 00:11:03,250
The Total instance averages all logical
processors, so one full core out of four

175
00:11:03,500 --> 00:11:05,100
adds about a quarter.

176
00:11:05,350 --> 00:11:06,550
Two: memory first.

177
00:11:06,800 --> 00:11:13,600
The paging is what keeps the disk busy, so
more RAM, or less memory use, calms the

178
00:11:13,850 --> 00:11:15,050
disk too.

179
00:11:15,300 --> 00:11:16,500
Three: about sixty.

180
00:11:16,750 --> 00:11:20,350
Five minutes is three hundred seconds,
divided by five.

181
00:11:20,600 --> 00:11:25,000
Four: add the Command line column in
Details, or use Get-CimInstance.

182
00:11:25,250 --> 00:11:28,050
The loop's command line contains while
true.

183
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:30,300
Did you get all four?

184
00:11:30,550 --> 00:11:32,550
Let's see it on SRV01.

185
00:11:32,800 --> 00:11:37,600
In Task Manager, the CPU page shows the
number of logical processors.

186
00:11:37,850 --> 00:11:41,050
On Details, I add the Command line column.

187
00:11:41,300 --> 00:11:46,100
In Performance Monitor, I add processor
time and Available MBytes, and switch

188
00:11:46,350 --> 00:11:47,950
views with Control G.

189
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:52,200
Here is SA1 Baseline, already recorded,
with its first report.

190
00:11:52,450 --> 00:11:56,450
Now I start a loop in a second PowerShell
window.

191
00:11:56,700 --> 00:11:57,900
CPU climbs.

192
00:11:58,150 --> 00:12:03,750
Task Manager sorts it to the top, and the
command line gives it away.

193
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,000
Resource Monitor filters to it.

194
00:12:06,250 --> 00:12:08,250
Get-Counter confirms the CPU value.

195
00:12:08,500 --> 00:12:11,300
End task, and the CPU drops back.

196
00:12:11,550 --> 00:12:12,750
To wrap up.

197
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,800
A baseline tells you what normal is.

198
00:12:16,050 --> 00:12:18,850
Watch CPU, memory, disk and network
together.

199
00:12:19,100 --> 00:12:22,700
Task Manager and Resource Monitor show the
present moment.

200
00:12:22,950 --> 00:12:27,350
Performance Monitor and data collector
sets show what happens over time.

201
00:12:27,600 --> 00:12:33,200
The rules of thumb are guidance, and
memory is the first thing to check.

202
00:12:33,450 --> 00:12:34,650
In Lab 9.

203
00:12:34,900 --> 00:12:40,900
1, about eighty-five minutes and practice
only, you use all of these tools on SRV01,

204
00:12:41,150 --> 00:12:47,150
record SA1 Baseline twice, once quiet and
once under a CPU loop you start yourself,

205
00:12:47,400 --> 00:12:52,600
compare the reports, practice with a
scheduled task of your own, and finish

206
00:12:52,850 --> 00:12:54,050
with Get-Counter.
